<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basava</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Basavanna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Basava, also called Basavanna, was a 12th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> poet, philosopher and social reformer and a minister at the court of the Kalachuri ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijjala-ii">Bijjala II</a> at Kalyani. He led the Virashaiva or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingayatism">Lingayat</a> movement, which rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> distinctions and temple ritual in favour of personal devotion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, and expressed its ideas in vachana poetry. He is revered as the founder of Lingayatism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basava.jpg" alt="Basava" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sscheral · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basava_Gaint_Statue_108_feet,_Basava_Kalyana.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
